WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.614
CI needs a machine that is nobody's laptop.

2
00:00:05.614 --> 00:00:11.173
One that runs the same commands for every push, and posts the result on the commit.

3
00:00:11.173 --> 00:00:13.719
GitHub Actions gives you that machine.

4
00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:16.583
Here is the chain that one git push sets off.

5
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:17.030
One.

6
00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:21.781
GitHub records an event: a push, of this commit, to this branch.

7
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:22.934
Two.

8
00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:30.744
GitHub reads the workflows folder in the commit you pushed, and starts a run of every workflow that listens for push.

9
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:31.406
Three.

10
00:00:31.406 --> 00:00:33.298
The run starts its jobs.

11
00:00:33.298 --> 00:00:36.372
This workflow has one job, called test.

12
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:36.883
Four.

13
00:00:36.883 --> 00:00:41.709
For each job, GitHub starts a new virtual machine, called the runner.

14
00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:48.050
It comes with Ubuntu and common tools, like git, Docker and Python.

15
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.624
But none of your files.

16
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:50.141
Five.

17
00:00:50.141 --> 00:00:55.725
A program on that machine, the runner application, runs the job's steps, in order.

18
00:00:55.725 --> 00:00:59.335
Each step is a ready-made action, or a shell command.

19
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:02.676
Checkout copies your repository onto the machine.

20
00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:05.565
Setup python sets up Python 3.14.

21
00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:08.427
Then pip installs the dependencies.

22
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.754
A step passes when its command exits with zero.

23
00:01:11.754 --> 00:01:13.499
Any other number fails it.

24
00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:16.282
pytest exits with 1 when a test fails.

25
00:01:16.282 --> 00:01:19.653
The job stops there, and the whole run turns red.

26
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.637
Here, every test passes.

27
00:01:21.637 --> 00:01:25.083
pytest exits with zero, and the job is green.

28
00:01:25.867 --> 00:01:28.352
A workflow can have more than one job.

29
00:01:28.352 --> 00:01:31.427
Jobs run in parallel, unless one needs another.

30
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.129
Lint and test start at the same moment, each on its own fresh machine.

31
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.542
Build says needs, lint and test.

32
00:01:38.542 --> 00:01:41.955
So it waits, and starts only after both succeed.

33
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:46.279
And no file moves from one job to the next, unless you save it.

34
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:47.690
Six.

35
00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:54.069
The result appears on the commit, and on its pull request: a green tick, or a red cross.

36
00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:56.535
Then GitHub deletes the machines.

37
00:01:56.535 --> 00:01:58.184
Nothing on them is kept.

38
00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:00.382
Your laptop took no part.

39
00:02:00.382 --> 00:02:01.611
It only pushed.

40
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.409
Push, event, workflow, a fresh runner for each job, steps in order, and the result on the commit.

41
00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:10.392
Then the machine is thrown away.
